"""Real-world applications for the advanced security-analysis lessons.

Each row is (lesson_id, case_id, connection, task, walkthrough). The applications are
original; quoted case context comes from the shared anchors in real_world_cases.py.
"""

APPLICATIONS = [
    # Core Security Analysis · Part IX
    ("1.25", "enron", "Accrual diagnostics matter most when reported profit and cash diverge while affiliated entities absorb the difference.", "Using the lesson's distributor example, compute the accrual ratio and cash conversion. Then list two receivables disclosures you would request from a company whose profit is rising faster than cash.", "The accrual ratio is 5.0% and cash conversion is 58.3%. Request the receivables aging and the customer-concentration note; both show whether the reported profit is supported by collectible third-party sales or by affiliated balances."),
    ("1.26", "csc", "A manipulation screen is only a prompt; the CSC case shows why the underlying accounting must be reconstructed from filings.", "Compute the lesson's M-score with TATA of 0.10 and state the screening conclusion. Then name the two line items you would reconcile first and why.", "The score is about −1.75, above the −1.78 threshold. Reconcile capitalized costs and revenue first: capitalization changes current profit, while revenue recognition changes the top line that the model is trying to test."),
    ("1.27", "lehman", "Hidden leverage is a central lesson of the 2008 crisis, but an analyst must distinguish reported debt from contingent and off-balance-sheet claims.", "Using the lesson's manufacturer, calculate reported and adjusted leverage. Then explain how a guarantee would be treated differently from a lease in a base case and a stress case.", "Reported leverage is 1.5× and adjusted leverage is 2.375×. The lease belongs in the base case because it is a contractual payment stream; the guarantee is contingent and belongs in a stress case unless the guaranteed party is already distressed."),
    ("1.28", "ge_disclosure", "Segment complexity can hide a loss-making unit or a concentrated profit source inside a healthy group average.", "Using the lesson's three-segment group, calculate HHI and segment profit. Then explain how a 35% customer concentration would change the stress case.", "HHI is 0.38; segment profits are $10M, $2.4M, and −$1.0M. A 35% customer would make the stress case depend on that customer's renewal and pricing, so the analyst should model a price cut or loss separately from the group average."),
    ("1.29", "bps_growth", "Normalization is the difference between a cyclical peak and a sustainable earning base, just as a GDP release distinguishes annual and quarterly comparisons.", "Using the lesson's five margins and $500M normalized revenue, calculate normalized EBIT and compare it with peak EBIT at 10% margin on $550M revenue.", "The average margin is 5.2% and normalized EBIT is $26M. Peak EBIT is $55M, more than twice the normalized figure. Valuing the peak as permanent overstates sustainable earning power."),
    ("1.30", "nikola", "Contingent claims and dilution require the analyst to ask what must happen before additional shares or payments appear.", "Using the lesson's 100M basic shares, 10M options at a $20 strike, and a $40 price, calculate diluted shares. Then describe how a contingent earn-out would be treated in a scenario.", "Net new shares are 5M, so diluted shares are 105M. A contingent earn-out belongs in a scenario with an explicit trigger and probability; it should not be added to the base share count as if it were certain."),
    ("1.31", "theranos", "A red-team capstone is designed to break the thesis before the market does; the Theranos case is a warning about evidence that never catches up with the narrative.", "Using the lesson's bear/base/bull values and 30% margin of safety, calculate the bear-case ceiling and compare it with the $9.00 price. Then write one falsification trigger for the base case.", "The bear-case ceiling is $3.50, far below $9.00. A falsification trigger could be two consecutive quarters of negative operating cash flow or the loss of the largest contract; either would move the case toward the bear scenario."),

    # Forensic accounting
    ("15.1", "enron", "The accrual-to-cash bridge is the first place an analyst sees that reported profit is not being collected.", "Using the lesson's distributor, calculate the current and prior cash conversion cycle. Then identify the account that explains most of the change and the note you would read next.", "Current CCC is 82.7 days versus 62.3 days prior, a 20.4-day deterioration driven mainly by receivables and inventory. Read the receivables aging and inventory note to see whether the change is growth, collection weakness, or obsolete stock."),
    ("15.2", "luckin", "Revenue red flags become concrete when receivables and contract assets grow faster than sales.", "Using the lesson's software company, calculate the change in DSO and contract-asset intensity. Then list two pieces of evidence that would distinguish a legitimate multi-year contract from premature recognition.", "DSO rises from 68.4 to 91.2 days and contract-asset intensity from 3.1% to 10.0%. Request the contract terms and customer acceptance evidence; also check whether cash collected from customers grew with revenue."),
    ("15.3", "csc", "Capitalization is the mechanical bridge between an operating cost and a balance-sheet asset, and it is a common fraud mechanism.", "Using the lesson's software company, calculate adjusted EBIT and the reported versus adjusted return on capital. Then explain why operating cash flow does not improve.", "Adjusted EBIT is $35M; reported return is 12.5% and adjusted return is lower on the adjusted capital base. Cash flow does not improve because the spending is still a cash outflow; capitalization only changes when the expense appears in profit."),
    ("15.4", "supermicro", "Inventory and reserve accounts are flexible estimates that can smooth profit across periods.", "Using the lesson's retailer, calculate inventory days and the reserve release's share of pre-tax profit. Then state what evidence would make the release acceptable.", "Inventory days rise from 136.9 to 182.5, and the release is 23% of pre-tax profit. The release is acceptable only if claims experience genuinely improved, the original reserve was reasonable, and the roll-forward supports the change."),
    ("15.5", "enron", "Related-party and special-purpose structures can move risk off the reported balance sheet without moving it away from the parent.", "Using the lesson's parent, reconstruct adjusted equity and the stress-case exposure. Then explain why the legal sale may not settle the economic risk.", "Adjusted equity is $70M after removing the $30M gain; the guarantee adds $25M of risk and the stress case adds the remaining $15M of SPE debt. The parent retains downside through the guarantee and buy-back, so the sale is not economically complete."),
    ("15.6", "theranos", "A weighted scorecard forces the analyst to state the evidence and weights before the conclusion.", "Using the lesson's five scores and weights, calculate the forensic score and identify the largest contributor. Then state one decision the committee could take besides rejecting the investment.", "The score is 3.55/5; related parties contribute the most. Besides rejection, the committee could require independent verification of the technology, a full related-party ledger, and a smaller position with a wider margin of safety."),

    # Credit and distress
    ("16.1", "ge_disclosure", "Coverage through the cycle is the credit analyst's first test of whether a complex issuer can service its obligations.", "Using the lesson's manufacturer, calculate base and trough interest coverage and FCF to debt. Then explain why EBITDA alone is insufficient.", "Base coverage is 4.0× and FCF/debt is 11.7%; trough coverage is 2.4× and trough FCF is −$5M. EBITDA ignores taxes, maintenance capex, and working capital, so it can overstate cash available to creditors."),
    ("16.2", "lehman", "A recovery waterfall turns a capital structure into a set of contingent claims on enterprise value.", "Using the lesson's pool and claims, calculate recovery for each tranche and identify the fulcrum security. Then explain how a $40M increase in the pool would change the answer.", "The secured claim is paid in full, unsecured recovers 66.7%, and subordinated recovers zero; the fulcrum is the senior unsecured note. A $40M larger pool would begin to pay the subordinated notes, moving the fulcrum."),
    ("16.3", "svb", "Covenants, liquidity, and maturities are three clocks, and the fastest one governs the credit outcome.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate current leverage, maximum debt, debt and EBITDA headroom, and liquidity runway. Then state which clock is most pressing.", "Leverage is 3.75× against a 4.0× covenant; maximum debt is $320M, so debt headroom is $20M and EBITDA headroom is $5M. Liquidity runway is 4.7 months. The liquidity clock is most pressing because cash runs out before the 2027 maturity."),
    ("16.4", "lehman", "A distressed valuation needs both a going-concern case and a liquidation floor, because they answer different questions.", "Using the lesson's retailer, calculate going-concern equity and the liquidation residual. Then explain which value anchors a secured-debt investment.", "Going-concern equity is $60M after restructuring; the liquidation residual is $3M. A secured-debt investor anchors on collateral recovery and the liquidation value, while the equity investor is buying the turnaround option."),
    ("16.5", "ge_disclosure", "A credit spread is compensation for expected loss plus risk and liquidity premiums, not a forecast of the coupon.", "Using the lesson's bond, estimate the promised yield and spread. Then calculate expected loss at an 8% default probability and 60% loss given default.", "The promised yield is about 11.9%, and the spread over 5% is about 6.9 points. Expected loss is 4.8%, leaving about 2.1 points for risk, liquidity, and estimation error. The investor should stress a higher default probability."),
    ("16.6", "ftx", "A distressed process starts with the claim map; a platform with related-party assets can have a very different recovery than its headline balance sheet suggests.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate recovery for the secured and unsecured tranches. Then explain how a one-year delay with $6M of additional fees changes the unsecured recovery.", "Proceeds are $70M; after $10M administration, secured recovers 100% and unsecured recovers nothing. A delay with $6M more fees reduces the pool further, so the unsecured claim remains out of the money."),

    # Quality and moats
    ("17.1", "bca_split", "ROIC and economic profit are the quality tests behind a valuation; a stock split changes the share count but not the economic profit.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate ROIC, economic profit, and value. Then explain why a stock split would not change any of those figures.", "ROIC is 15%, economic profit is $25M, and value is $916.7M. A split changes the number of shares and the per-share price, not NOPAT, invested capital, or economic profit."),
    ("17.2", "warung", "A moat is a structural reason returns persist; a neighborhood shop's advantage may come from location, trust, or switching costs.", "Using the lesson's scoring method, assign evidence and durability scores to a fictional shop's location and customer relationships. Then state what evidence would weaken the score.", "A location advantage might score 4 for evidence and 3 for durability; customer trust might score 3 and 3. A new competitor, a rent increase, or a change in foot traffic would weaken the location score and should be monitored."),
    ("17.3", "bca_split", "Reinvestment runway and incremental returns determine whether growth creates value, independent of share-count changes.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate the reinvestment rate, implied growth, and value. Then calculate value if incremental ROIC falls to 10%.", "The reinvestment rate is 40%, growth is 8%, and value is $3,000M at a 20% incremental return. At a 10% incremental return, growth is 4% and value falls to $1,000M because new capital earns only the cost of capital."),
    ("17.4", "ge_disclosure", "Capital allocation is a per-share decision, and complex groups can destroy value through acquisitions or buybacks at the wrong price.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate value per share after a buyback at $8 and at $12. Then state which price creates value if intrinsic value is $10.", "At $8, the company retires 20M shares and value per share rises to $10.50; at $12, it retires 13.33M shares and value falls to $9.69. The $8 buyback creates value; the $12 buyback destroys it relative to the $10 intrinsic estimate."),
    ("17.5", "theranos", "Governance and incentives determine whose interests the controller is likely to serve when the evidence is ambiguous.", "Using the lesson's scores, calculate the equal-weighted governance score. Then list two governance documents you would request from a private company.", "The score is 1.4/5. Request the board minutes and the related-party transaction ledger; for a private company, also request the investor rights agreement and the terms of the last financing round."),
    ("17.6", "nikola", "A quality scorecard should be paired with disconfirming evidence, especially when the story depends on partnerships and backlog.", "Using the lesson's scores and weights, calculate the quality score and the largest contributor. Then state one disconfirming observation for the moat score.", "The score is 3.55/5; reinvestment is the largest contributor. A disconfirming observation for the moat would be a competitor launching a comparable product at a lower price, or the loss of the partnership that supports the backlog."),

    # Banks
    ("18.1", "svb", "A bank's net interest margin is a spread business, and the margin must be connected to the risk taken to earn it.", "Using the lesson's bank, calculate NIM and pre-tax income. Then explain why a high NIM is not automatically a quality signal.", "NIM is 3.0% and pre-tax income is $13M. A high NIM can reflect riskier lending or a duration mismatch; the analyst must connect it to credit quality, funding stability, and liquidity."),
    ("18.2", "svb", "Asset quality is the bank's largest credit risk, and provisioning adequacy determines how much capital a shock consumes.", "Using the lesson's bank, calculate the NPL ratio, coverage, and credit cost. Then calculate the additional allowance if NPLs double and target coverage is 70%.", "The NPL ratio is 5.0%, coverage is 60.0%, and credit cost is 1.0%. If NPLs double to $80M and coverage is 70%, required allowance is $56M, so additional provisions are $32M before charge-offs."),
    ("18.3", "svb", "Capital ratios constrain growth and distributions, and risk-weighted assets can rise faster than the balance sheet.", "Using the lesson's bank, calculate the CET1 and leverage ratios. Then calculate capital headroom over a 7% CET1 requirement.", "CET1 is 8.0% and leverage is 7.5%. At a 7% requirement, required capital is $70M, so headroom is $10M. A rise in RWA or a credit loss could eliminate that headroom quickly."),
    ("18.4", "svb", "Liquidity and deposit concentration can fail a bank before its capital ratio looks inadequate.", "Using the lesson's bank, calculate the LCR and top-10 deposit share. Then calculate the LCR if half of the top-ten deposits leave.", "LCR is 120% and the top-10 share is 30%. If half of the top-ten deposits leave, outflows rise by $150M to $300M, and LCR falls to 60%, forcing asset sales or new funding."),
    ("18.5", "ge_disclosure", "Insurance reserves and combined ratios require the same discipline as bank provisioning: estimates of future claims drive current profit.", "Using the lesson's insurer, calculate the combined ratio, underwriting profit, and pre-tax income. Then calculate the combined ratio with $20M of adverse development.", "The combined ratio is 90%, underwriting profit is $10M, and pre-tax income is $38M. With $20M of adverse development, the combined ratio becomes 110% and underwriting becomes a $10M loss."),
    ("18.6", "bca_split", "A bank's P/B multiple is a function of ROE, cost of equity, and growth, not of its share price history or split-adjusted quote.", "Using the lesson's bank, calculate the justified P/B and equity value. Then calculate P/B if ROE falls to 8%.", "At a 12% ROE, 10% cost of equity, and 3% growth, justified P/B is 1.286 and value is $128.6M. At an 8% ROE, P/B falls to 0.714, a discount to book because the bank earns below its cost of equity."),

    # Valuation under uncertainty
    ("19.1", "warung", "Scenario DCF forces the analyst to describe the operating conditions behind each case, not just change the discount rate.", "Using the lesson's scenarios, calculate each EV and the expected EV. Then explain why the expected value can exceed the base case.", "The bear, base, and bull EVs are $707M, $1,457.1M, and $2,422.7M; expected EV is $1,511.0M. The expected value exceeds the base case because the bull case is larger than the bear case at equal probability."),
    ("19.2", "negative_prices", "Sensitivity analysis shows which assumptions matter, and some market variables can move outside historical ranges.", "Using the lesson's table, identify the input with the largest value swing. Then explain what a negative-price scenario would do to a valuation that assumes a floor at zero.", "The WACC has the largest swing at $429.2M. A negative-price scenario would invalidate a zero-floor assumption; the analyst should model the contractual or operational exposure directly rather than treat the variable as bounded."),
    ("19.3", "bi_rate", "Real options appear whenever a decision can be staged or deferred, including investment decisions that depend on interest-rate conditions.", "Using the lesson's project, calculate static NPV and option value. Then explain why waiting can be valuable even when the static NPV is positive.", "Static NPV is $23.6M; the option to wait is worth $54.5M. Waiting is valuable because it avoids the bad-state loss; the analyst should compare the option value with the cost of delay and the risk of losing the opportunity."),
    ("19.4", "gme", "A reverse DCF turns a price into an implied expectation, which is useful when the market narrative is strong.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate the implied perpetual growth rate. Then explain what the result would mean if the company's end market is shrinking.", "Implied growth is 3.55%. If the end market is shrinking, that expectation is too optimistic; the analyst should solve for revenue growth or margin instead of assuming perpetual growth, and compare the result with the company's history."),
    ("19.5", "svb", "Distress-adjusted valuation separates the going-concern value from the probability and cost of a failed state.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate expected EV and expected equity. Then compare with the naive going-concern equity value.", "Expected EV is $440M; expected equity is $140M before expected distress costs and $134M after them. The naive going-concern equity is $200M, which overstates value by ignoring the distress state."),
    ("19.6", "factors", "Valuation discipline requires the price ceiling to be fixed before the market price influences the estimate.", "Using the lesson's conservative value and 30% requirement, calculate the maximum price. Then calculate the actual margin at $35 and state the decision.", "The maximum price is $28.00. At $35, the margin to the conservative value is 12.5%, but the price is 25% above the ceiling, so the purchase is rejected. The analyst should not raise the conservative value without new evidence."),

    # Cyclicals and commodities
    ("20.1", "bps_growth", "Mid-cycle normalization separates the sustainable earnings base from the current point in the economic cycle.", "Using the lesson's steel producer, calculate the average margin and normalized EBIT. Then compare the peak and normalized valuations at a 7× multiple.", "The average margin is 5.6% and normalized EBIT is $44.8M. At 7×, normalized value is $313.6M versus $756M on peak EBIT; capitalizing the peak overstates value by more than 140%."),
    ("20.2", "negative_prices", "Commodity exposure and hedging determine how a price shock flows into revenue and cash flow.", "Using the lesson's producer, calculate realized price and EBIT at spot $50 and at spot $40, with the 60% hedge. Then explain the hedge's effect on upside and downside.", "At $50, realized price is $48.80 and EBIT is $1.38M; at $40, realized price is $44.80 and EBIT is $0.98M. The hedge reduces upside relative to spot but protects $0.48M of EBIT in the stress."),
    ("20.3", "bi_rate", "Operating leverage describes how a change in volume affects profit, and it cuts both ways.", "Using the lesson's manufacturer, calculate contribution margin, EBIT, and DOL. Then calculate EBIT if revenue falls 10%.", "Contribution is $60M, EBIT is $20M, and DOL is 3.0×. A 10% revenue decline lowers EBIT to $14M, a 30% decline. The analyst should check whether fixed costs can be cut in a downturn."),
    ("20.4", "warung", "Working-capital cycles connect growth to cash, especially in a business that buys inventory before it collects from customers.", "Using the lesson's machinery maker, calculate the cash conversion cycle and the incremental working capital at 20% growth.", "CCC is 82.7 days. At 20% growth with constant ratios, net working capital rises from $100M to $120M, requiring $20M of cash before the new revenue produces profit."),
    ("20.5", "bca_split", "Capital intensity and maintenance capex determine how much cash a business must reinvest to stand still.", "Using the lesson's manufacturer, calculate maintenance free cash flow and growth capex. Then explain why depreciation is an imperfect proxy.", "Maintenance FCF is $60M and growth capex is $40M. Depreciation is an accounting estimate; a company can defer maintenance or depreciate assets over lives that do not match the actual spending required."),
    ("20.6", "ge_disclosure", "Balance-sheet resilience is tested by stressing EBITDA, liquidity, covenants, and capex together.", "Using the lesson's mining company, calculate base and stressed leverage and the trough cash flow. Then state the first action management should consider.", "Base leverage is 2.0× and stressed leverage is 3.33× against a 3.5× covenant. Trough cash flow before working capital is $40M; after a $60M working-capital build it is −$20M. Management should cut growth capex or suspend the dividend first."),

    # Technology and intangibles
    ("21.1", "csc", "R&D and software capitalization change reported profit and capital, so comparability requires an adjustment.", "Using the lesson's software company, calculate adjusted EBIT and reported versus adjusted return on capital. Then state why the adjustment is not a claim that all R&D is an asset.", "Adjusted EBIT is $75M; reported return is 37.5% and adjusted return is 13.6%. The adjustment is a comparability tool, not an assertion that every research dollar creates a durable asset; the analyst should test the amortization period and the project outcomes."),
    ("21.2", "ml_pipeline", "Platform unit economics connect acquisition spending to the lifetime gross profit of a customer.", "Using the lesson's platform, calculate LTV, CAC payback, and LTV/CAC. Then recalculate LTV if churn rises to 30%.", "LTV is $240, payback is 2.08 years, and LTV/CAC is 2.4×. At 30% churn, LTV falls to $160 and the ratio to 1.6×, which may not justify the acquisition spending."),
    ("21.3", "ml_pipeline", "Cohort analysis tests whether a platform's retention is improving or whether growth is masking churn.", "Using the lesson's cohort, calculate gross retention and NRR for years 1 and 2. Then explain what the pattern suggests about the network effect.", "Gross retention is 80% in year 1; NRR is 88% in year 1 and 69% in year 2. The declining NRR suggests that expansion is not offsetting churn, so the network-effect claim needs more evidence."),
    ("21.4", "nikola", "Intangible-heavy comparables require consistent adjustments for R&D, stock compensation, and growth.", "Using the lesson's companies, calculate EV/revenue, EV/gross profit, and adjusted EBIT for each. Then state which comparison is most informative.", "Company A trades at 5.0× revenue and 6.25× gross profit with negative adjusted EBIT; Company B trades at 4.0× and 5.71× with $30M of adjusted EBIT. The EV/adjusted EBIT comparison is most informative if the businesses are at similar maturity."),
    ("21.5", "theranos", "Evidence scores separate verifiable operating facts from promotional claims before a high multiple is paid.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate the equal-weighted evidence score. Then list two documents you would request before relying on the growth story.", "The score is 1.6/5. Request the audited financial statements and the executed partnership agreement, plus customer references and the related-party ledger. The analyst should also check whether the counterparty has disclosed the partnership."),
    ("21.6", "gme", "Growth valuation is a claim about duration and returns, not only about the current revenue multiple.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate EV/revenue and mature NOPAT. Then explain why the current price depends on the path, not only the endpoint.", "EV/revenue is 12× and mature NOPAT is $150M. The price depends on the growth path, reinvestment, dilution, and the terminal value after year eight; a high endpoint alone does not justify the multiple."),

    # Real-world casebook
    ("22.1", "enron", "The Enron case is a study in the gap between legal structure and economic substance.", "Using the lesson's parent, reconstruct adjusted equity and the guarantee exposure. Then state the three questions you would ask about the affiliated entity.", "Adjusted equity is $30M after removing the $40M gain and deducting the $30M guarantee. Ask who controls the entity, whether the sale price was arm's length, and whether the parent retains a buy-back or liquidity obligation."),
    ("22.2", "csc", "The CSC and WorldCom cases show how capitalizing operating costs can defer losses and eventually force a restatement.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate adjusted EBIT and the current profit boost. Then explain what happens to equity when the accounting is corrected.", "Adjusted EBIT is $70M and the current profit boost is $80M. When the accounting is corrected, the unamortized balance is written off against equity, and prior-period profits may be restated; covenants and liquidity can also be affected."),
    ("22.3", "luckin", "The Luckin case shows why revenue must be reconciled to cash and separated from related-party sales.", "Using the lesson's company, calculate third-party revenue and cash conversion on both bases. Then explain why the related-party revenue should be excluded from a sustainable base.", "Third-party revenue is $350M; cash conversion is 60% on total revenue and 85.7% on third-party revenue. The related-party revenue may not be at arm's length or collectible, so excluding it gives a more defensible sustainable base."),
    ("22.4", "theranos", "The Theranos case is a study in private valuations that outrun the evidence.", "Using the lesson's cases, calculate the bear, base, and bull values and the gap to the $9B headline. Then list the evidence you would request.", "The values are $50M, $240M, and $600M; even the bull case is $8.4B below the headline. Request audited financials, customer contracts, independent technical validation, and the terms of the last financing round."),
    ("22.5", "nikola", "The Nikola case shows how partnerships and backlog can be presented as revenue evidence when they are not binding.", "Using the lesson's backlog categories, calculate the evidence-backed backlog and the narrative premium. Then state one document you would request for the conditional contract.", "Evidence-backed backlog is $2.4B and the narrative premium is $7.6B. Request the conditional customer's financing commitment and the contract's termination and volume provisions."),
    ("22.6", "ftx", "The FTX case is a study in customer-asset segregation, related-party receivables, and governance.", "Using the lesson's platform, calculate headline coverage, liquid coverage, and the stress shortfall. Then state the first governance question you would ask.", "Headline coverage is 100%, liquid coverage is 20%, and the stress shortfall is $80B. The first governance question is who controls the affiliated entities that owe the platform money and whether customer assets are legally segregated."),
    ("22.7", "svb", "SVB and Lehman show that solvency and liquidity can fail on different clocks, and a run can convert one into the other.", "Using the lesson's bank, estimate the mark-to-market loss and compare it with tangible equity. Then explain how a run by uninsured depositors makes the loss real.", "The loss is about $15B against $10B of tangible equity. A run forces the bank to sell securities at a loss, converting an unrealized loss into a realized one and reducing capital, which can trigger further withdrawals."),
    ("22.8", "ge_disclosure", "GE's complexity shows why segment and reserve analysis are essential before accepting a consolidated result.", "Using the lesson's conglomerate, reconcile group EBIT from the segments and calculate the insurance charge's share. Then explain why the charge may not be nonrecurring.", "Segment profits sum to $160M; after the $40M charge, group EBIT is $120M, so the charge is 33.3% of group EBIT. The charge may not be nonrecurring if the reserve increase reflects a systematic underestimation of claims, so the analyst should read the reserve roll-forward."),
]
